# Chapter 4, eighth part: the local formats and the messages of the panels.
# Part of the chapter source.

.h2 Local formats [format-local]
.ix Local format
.ix Local formats
.ix Current format
.ix F6, Format
.ix Ctrl-E, Block Format Editor
.ix Block Format Editor
.ix Resetting local formats
A local format belongs to one piece of a document: a run of characters, one
block, or one column of a table.  It is looked up before any global setting
({n:format-order}) and so it wins over all of them.  The local formats are
made with a few keys and one panel, and they are described by the piece
of text that they format: a block or a column ({n:format-local-block}) and
characters ({n:format-local-text}).
.table 16 21 20
Piece | How it is made | Applies to
Characters | Attribute keys, {x:format-local-text} | The text after the mark.
Block | {k:F6} or {k:Ctrl-E}, {k:Ctrl-H} | The block.
Column of a table | {k:F6} or {k:Ctrl-E} in the column | The column.
.end
{x:edit} describes how the commands of the edit screen are chosen;
this section describes only what they do to the format.  The width of a column
and the gaps at its sides are set by the Table commands of {n:edit}, not by
{k:F6}.

.h3 The format of a block or of a column [format-local-block]
.ix Block format, local
.ix Column format, local
.ix Left block indent, local
.ix Format of one block
Press {k:F6} (Format), or {k:Ctrl-E}, with the cursor in a block of the
document.  The two keys do the same thing.  A panel called Block Format
Editor puts five questions one after the other, each answered with
{k:Enter}; the question shows the value that the block has now, as the lookup
of {x:format-order} finds it, so that {k:Enter} alone keeps it.
.table 5 18 34
No | Question | Answers
1 | L: left block indent (ruler units) | A number typed in a text line.
2 | Justification | A list: Left, Right, Center, Even.
3 | Indent | A list: None, First, All but First, All.
4 | Attributes | A list: Normal, Bold, Italics, Bold Italics, Underscore Words.
5 | Tabs (e.g. 5,20R,40D; 0 = none) | A text line of tab stops.
.end
The text lines are edited with the usual keys of a one line box ({k:Left},
{k:Right}, {k:Home}, {k:End}, {k:Backspace}, {k:Del}); the lists are moved
with {k:Up}, {k:Down}, {k:Home}, {k:End}, {k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn} and the
highlight starts on the value that the block has.  The last box shows the
tab stops of the block in the form that it takes back, for example
{c:5,20R}, so that a stop can be changed in place.  The stops are described
in {x:format-set-tabs}.
.table 7 53
Key | Effect
{k:Enter} | Accept the answer and go on to the next question; after the last
 question, make the change.
{k:Esc} | In any question: leave the panel.  Nothing is changed, not even
 what was answered before.
.end
The change is made when the fifth answer is accepted, and then all of it is
made together.  A left block indent that is typed with letters is read as far
as it goes: a text with no digit at the start is 0.  In a block of several
columns (a table) the answers go to the column that holds the cursor; the
left block indent belongs to the block, so the answer to the first question
is read only in a block of one column.  The document is marked as
changed, the block is drawn again with its new format and the ruler follows
it at once.  When the cursor is not in a block no question is asked.
.note A block that has gone through the panel and been accepted has a local
  setting for each of the four answers, even for those that it did not
  change, and no later change of the global setting reaches it.  A block that
  has never been in the panel follows the global settings.
.h4 Hanging indent
.ix Hanging indent, local
.ix Ctrl-H, hanging indent
{k:Ctrl-H} makes a hanging indent for the block that holds the cursor: the
first tab stop of the block is put where the cursor stands, counted from the
left block indent, and the Indent is set to All but First.  The message line
shows "Hanging indent set at N", N being the position.  The indents are
described in {x:format-set-indents}.

.h3 Characters [format-local-text]
.ix Text format, local
.ix Attribute marks
.ix Attribute keys, local format
.ix Set Attributes
The format of characters is a mark in the text itself: the attribute that
applies from that point to the next mark.  It is made with the keys of
{x:format-screen-attrkeys}: {k:Ctrl-B} for bold, {k:Alt-I} for
italics, {k:Ctrl-U} for underscored words, {k:Ctrl-N} for normal, and
{k:Alt-F6} for the list Set Attributes (Normal, Bold, Italics, Underscore
Words).  Each key puts a mark at the cursor that turns its attribute on when
it was off and off when it was on, so that the text that is typed next has the
attribute; with text highlighted the commands of {n:edit} apply it to the
highlighted text.  The mark is added to the attribute of the block's format and
cannot take it away (the Behavior in {n:format-order}): a bold block
cannot be made normal word by word, only by changing the format of the block.
.table 15 45
Settings | Which one wins
Block says Bold, text says Normal | Bold: the text adds, never removes.
Block says Underscore, text says Bold | Bold and underscored.
Block says Normal, text says Italics | Italics from the mark to the next mark.
.end

.h2 Messages of the format panels [format-messages]
.ix Messages, format panels
.ix Error messages, Global menu
.ix Panel messages, Global menu
.ix Refused value
.ix Refused value, Global menu
.ix Messages, F6 panel
Every panel of the Global menu keeps what it is given until Ins is pressed,
and then looks at all the values together.  A wrong value is refused with a
box that has a title and one line of text.  The box is a notice: any key closes
it, the panel that holds the fault is shown again, nothing has been applied,
and the document has not been changed.  The tables below give the text of
every message that these panels and the {k:F6} panel can show, the title of the
box, and what to do.  The parts in capitals vary.
.h3 Global Format [format-messages-format]
.ix Format, messages
.table 23 10 24
Text | Title | What it means
FIELD must be a number such as 1.50 | Format | A spacing field, or Distance, is
 | | not a number.
FIELD must be a whole number | Format | A whole number field has a sign,
 | | a point or a letter.
FIELD is a number from 0 to 99 | Format | Table of Contents Level is not a
 | | whole number from 0 to 99.
FIELD must be a number from 1 to 99 | Format | Number Every is not from 1 to 99.
FIELD must be from 0 to 2.00 inches | Format | Distance is more than 2.00.
.end
FIELD is the label of the field: Line Spacing, Headline Spacing, Block Spacing,
Subsection Spacing, Peer Section Spacing or Distance for the first message;
Indent per Level, Page Break Level, Page Number Level, Auto Numbering to Level,
Number Every, Winding Columns or Indent to Next Level for the second; Table of
Contents Level for the third; Number Every for the fourth and Distance for the
fifth.  The first fault in the order of the fourteen panels is reported, and
that panel is shown.  Correct the field and press {k:Ins}
again.  The rules of a good number are in {x:format-format-apply}.
.h3 Page [format-messages-page]
.ix Page Layout, messages
.table 23 11 23
Text | Title | What it means
FIELD must be a number such as 1.000 | Page Layout | A margin is not a number
 | | or has more than three decimals.
FIELD must be 0 to 100 | Page Layout | Vertical Centering is not a whole
 | | number from 0 to 100.
.end
The Page Number Tag and the Paper Size are not checked (the paper is chosen
from a list).  The rules are in {x:format-page-checks}.
.h3 Named [format-messages-named]
.ix Named Block Format, messages
.ix Delete Named Block, messages
.table 21 13 23
Text | Title | What it means
Enter a name to save the format as | Named Block Format | Save as is empty.
Invalid number | Named Block Format | A name with a control character,
 | | or a Block Indent (Left) with a
 | | character that is not a digit.
There are no named block formats | Delete Named Block | Delete was chosen and
 | | the document has none.
.end
The question "Delete the named block format NAME?" is a list of Yes and No,
not a notice ({n:format-named-delete}).
.h3 Keycap Format [format-messages-keycap]
.ix Keycap Format, messages
.table 43 17
Text | Title
Foreground and Background are 0.0 to 100.0, Scale is 0.100 to 10.000 | Keycap
 | Format
.end
The Marker group has one message.  The others of the group (the
note, counter and display panels) refuse nothing, as {x:format-marker-notes}
says.  The text shown is the one in the table; the box is a notice, the Marker
panel stays, and the values are not applied until all are right.  The messages
that the panel of one marker gives (Marker, Alt-F12) are those of {n:markers}.
.h3 Options and the other files of the user [format-messages-options]
.ix Options, messages
.ix Config file, error
.ix Settings, could not be saved
When Options is accepted the five settings take effect and the file
{c:config} of the user is written.  If that fails a box titled Options gives
the reason, and the settings keep their value until the Editor ends.
.table 31 29
Text | What it means
There is no home directory | No home directory is known and {c:STRATA_HOME} is not
 set.
Cannot make DIR: REASON | The directory of the settings could not be made.
DIR is not a directory of yours | The directory is a link, or belongs to
 somebody else.  It is not used.
Cannot write FILE: REASON | The file cannot be written;
 the reason is the one of the system (a full disk,
 a directory that cannot be written).
.end
The same four messages belong to the list of fonts of the Document Manager,
which has the title Fonts there ({n:str}), in a slightly different wording.
.h3 The F6 panel and the keys of the formats [format-messages-keys]
.ix Messages, format keys
.ix Hanging indent, messages
These are not boxes: they are written in the message line of the screen and
stay until the next key.
.table 31 29
Text | When
Hanging indent set at N | {k:Ctrl-H}, N is the position of the stop.
Type the hanging characters first, then | {k:Ctrl-H} with the cursor at the left
 press {k:Ctrl-H} | block indent.
.end
